# Regression test: `source`-ing several files in a single statement (`source $a $b
# $c ...`) used to deadlock, the same root cause as tests/source_three_statements.crush
# one level up: the `source` builtin (`src/builtins/control/mod.rs`) passes its own
# `context.output` -- a real, bounded(1) channel, since this statement's own result is
# read exactly once by `Job::eval` -- unchanged to `execute::file` for *every* file in
# the list. Once `execute::file`/`source()` (src/lang/execute.rs) only forwards the
# last statement of *its own* file (the fix for the test above), each file here still
# has just one statement, so each one *is* that file's own last statement -- meaning
# each file still tries to send its own value onto that same shared channel, with
# nothing ever draining it in between. Confirmed directly: 2 files alone weren't always
# enough to observe this (a little slack elsewhere absorbs the first couple of sends),
# but it reproduces reliably from 4+ files on -- hence 5 here, with margin.
#
# `echo` prints directly (bypassing the output sender entirely -- see its own doc
# comment) before ever touching its own return value, so each file's visible output
# still appears even while hung: a hang here shows up as "a".."e" printed followed by
# the process simply never exiting, caught by tests/system.rs's own 30-second
# per-test hang-detection timeout regardless of what output was expected.
$f1 := $(convert $file $("/tmp/source_multiple_files_repro_a_{}".format $($random.integer 1000000000)))
$f2 := $(convert $file $("/tmp/source_multiple_files_repro_b_{}".format $($random.integer 1000000000)))
$f3 := $(convert $file $("/tmp/source_multiple_files_repro_c_{}".format $($random.integer 1000000000)))
$f4 := $(convert $file $("/tmp/source_multiple_files_repro_d_{}".format $($random.integer 1000000000)))
$f5 := $(convert $file $("/tmp/source_multiple_files_repro_e_{}".format $($random.integer 1000000000)))
cmd rm "-f" $f1
cmd rm "-f" $f2
cmd rm "-f" $f3
cmd rm "-f" $f4
cmd rm "-f" $f5
$bin.from "echo a\n" | $bin.to $f1
$bin.from "echo b\n" | $bin.to $f2
$bin.from "echo c\n" | $bin.to $f3
$bin.from "echo d\n" | $bin.to $f4
$bin.from "echo e\n" | $bin.to $f5
source $f1 $f2 $f3 $f4 $f5
cmd rm "-f" $f1
cmd rm "-f" $f2
cmd rm "-f" $f3
cmd rm "-f" $f4
cmd rm "-f" $f5
